// Parameters used to specify behavioral responses in tax policy analysis.
//
// This JSON file serves as a template for specifying behavioral response
// parameters that specify how employers/individuals respond to a tax reform.
// All four keys must be present; use 0.0 to turn off a response channel.
//
//   "esf" : earnings shift factor is the fraction of the reform-induced
//           increase (decrease) in the employer payroll tax liability that
//           is shifted to the employee as a decrease (increase) in earnings
//           with the remainder shifted to the employee as a decrease (increase)
//           in non-taxable benefits such as employer-provided health insurance;
//           the esf valid range is [0, 1]; empirical estimate is typically
//           0.85.  Note that any earnings shift is applied BEFORE the
//           remaining elasticity calculations (see below) are performed.
//
//   "sub" : substitution elasticity of taxable income; zero or positive;
//           empirical estimates are typically in the 0.1 to 0.4 range.
//
//   "inc" : income elasticity of taxable income; zero or negative;
//           values used in practice are typically in the 0.0 to -0.2 range.
//
//   "cg"  : SEMI-elasticity of long-term capital gains; zero or negative.
//           This is NOT the tax-rate elasticity usually reported in the
//           literature: a tax-rate elasticity of -0.792 corresponds to a
//           "cg" value of about -3.45.
//
// Detailed documentation on these parameters, including their exact
// definitions and the response equations in which they are used, can be
// found at the following URLs:
// https://taxcalc.pslmodels.org/guide/behavior_params.html
// https://taxcalc.pslmodels.org/api/behresp.html
{
    "esf": 0.0,
    "sub": 0.0,
    "inc": 0.0,
    "cg": 0.0
}
